     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 20 mg is double the FDA-labeled maximum for amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Going above that dose usually adds more peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia without much more blood pressure lowering.\n\nDid you mean one of these?\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily**, the maximum dose.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily**, the usual starting dose. Some patients start at 2.5 mg, such as frail or older patients or those with hepatic impairment.\n- If 10 mg isn't controlling the blood pressure, the usual next step is to add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide-type diuretic, rather than raising the amlodipine.\n\nIf you really do want 20 mg, for example because your attending or a specialist recommended it, tell me and I'll enter it with a note documenting that. Please check with your supervising physician first.\n\nTell me which dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
